            <ArticleTitle>Anomalous bronchial artery originating from the right coronary artery in a patient with angina (2009: 4b).</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1822-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00330-008-1145-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Bronchial artery origins are subject to a wide range of anatomic variations, of which interventional radiologists should be aware. The authors report a patient with angina in whom an anomalous bronchial artery originated from the sinus node branch of the right coronary artery, causing a coronary steal phenomenon. The patient's symptom was successfully treated by transcatheter embolisation of the anomalous bronchial artery, which seems to be an effective alternative to surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
